<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    WARNINGS AND NOTICES ARE FAILURES, AND THEIR DETAILS ARE PRINTED.

    Warning failure and issue details began on 2026-08-13. Notice failure begins
    with this measured change. The original cost was concrete: CI reported
    "success" on every run while PHPUnit classified 1347 of 1407 tests as
    warnings rather than passes. Nothing in the local workflow showed it — the
    summary reads "passed" — so the suite looked clean from the only place
    anyone was looking.

    The cause was a single suppressed vendor call: phpdotenv reads .env with
    `@file_get_contents()` and handles a missing file itself, but PHPUnit's
    error handler does not honour `@`, so every application boot in an
    environment without a .env became a test warning. CI had no .env by design.
    It now creates an empty one, and this file refuses to call a warning a pass.

    displayDetailsOnAllIssues is what makes an issue actionable rather than a
    badge: the message and the originating file:line are printed. failOnWarning
    and failOnNotice stop either category from hiding behind a green run for a
    phase.

    ALL ISSUES, RATHER THAN A LIST OF CATEGORIES. This began as four
    displayDetailsOnTestsThatTrigger* flags, and cross-review found the gap that
    shape creates: PHPUnit separates issues IT raises from issues the code under
    test raises, so displayDetailsOnPhpunitNotices and
    displayDetailsOnPhpunitDeprecations were still false while the comment here
    claimed everything was displayed. A hand-maintained list of categories drifts
    exactly the way a hand-maintained list of method names does — the mistake
    ActionBoundaryArchTest already documents. One attribute covers every category
    PHPUnit has now and any it adds later, and it cannot be true of some
    categories and false of others.

    NOTICES NOW FAIL, AFTER THEY WERE COUNTED IN BOTH PAO MODES. The same full
    suite run reports zero notices and zero warnings with PAO's condensed output
    and with PAO_DISABLE=true, so this does not hide a known issue behind a new
    gate. Deprecations still print but do not fail: they have not yet received
    the same two-mode measurement.

    WarningGateTest proves the gates rather than trusting this file: it runs a
    child PHPUnit over probes that trigger a warning and a notice, with the
    attributes read from THIS file, and asserts each run exits non-zero — then
    flips the corresponding flag off and asserts the same probe passes.
-->
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="vendor/phpunit/phpunit/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="tests/bootstrap.php"
         colors="true"

         displayDetailsOnAllIssues="true"

         failOnWarning="true"
         failOnNotice="true"
>
    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="Unit">
            <directory>tests/Unit</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="Feature">
            <directory>tests/Feature</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>
    <source>
        <include>
            <directory>app</directory>
        </include>
    </source>
    <php>
        <!--
            PHP's 128M default is no longer enough to run this suite in one
            process. P1-T11 took it past the line: the certificate upload tests
            build multi-megabyte strings to exercise the size ceiling, and by the
            time they run the accumulated fixtures tip the process over, so the
            suite dies mid-run with "Allowed memory size exhausted" in a file that
            passes perfectly well on its own.

            Raised here rather than left to a command-line flag, because
            `php artisan test` spawns its own process and -d on the parent does
            not reach it — the limit has to come from configuration or not at all.
        -->
        <ini name="memory_limit" value="512M"/>
        <env name="APP_ENV" value="testing"/>
        <env name="APP_MAINTENANCE_DRIVER" value="file"/>
        <env name="BCRYPT_ROUNDS" value="4"/>
        <env name="BROADCAST_CONNECTION" value="null"/>
        <env name="CACHE_STORE" value="array"/>
        <env name="DB_CONNECTION" value="mysql"/>
        <env name="DB_DATABASE" value="training_center_test"/>
        <env name="DB_URL" value=""/>
        <env name="MAIL_MAILER" value="array"/>
        <env name="QUEUE_CONNECTION" value="sync"/>
        <env name="SESSION_DRIVER" value="array"/>
        <env name="PULSE_ENABLED" value="false"/>
        <env name="TELESCOPE_ENABLED" value="false"/>
        <env name="NIGHTWATCH_ENABLED" value="false"/>
    </php>
</phpunit>
